INSERT INTO blog_posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, author, status, cover_image, published_date, post_date, created_at, updated_at)
  VALUES (
    'The Impact of a ''Fertilizer Shock'' on Healthy Eating and Grocers',
    'the-impact-of-a-fertilizer-shock-on-healthy-eating-and-grocers',
    'The fertilizer shock will have a major and immediate impact on produce and other plant-based healthy foods. Grocers will need to act quick and then continuously monitor the situation to meet shoppers'' needs.',
    $$<p>The Sage Take: The fertilizer shock will have a major and immediate impact on produce and other plant-based healthy foods. Grocers will need to act quick and then continuously monitor the situation to meet shoppers' needs.</p><h3>The Situation: Fertilizer Becomes a Grocery Question</h3><p>All politics aside, the facts are that disruptions around the <a href="https://theconversation.com/hormuz-closure-threatens-the-global-food-supply-why-grocery-price-hikes-are-coming-279899" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Strait of Hormuz</a> have hit one of the world’s most important corridors for <a href="https://unctad.org/news/gas-grain-fertilizer-disruptions-raise-risks-food-security-and-trade" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">fertilizer</a>. According to <a href="https://unctad.org/news/hormuz-disruption-shows-why-early-warning-data-matters" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNCTAD</a> and <a href="https://www.ifpri.org/blog/the-iran-wars-impacts-on-global-fertilizer-markets-and-food-production/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IFPRI</a>, a large share of globally traded nitrogen and phosphate fertilizers now faces higher costs, delays, or rerouting just as farmers make planting decisions. Those disruptions matter because higher fertilizer and energy costs do not stay on the farm; they move into grocery through grains, feed, produce, and packaged food ingredients.</p><p>The result is not one clean price shock across the store. The near-term squeeze is more likely to show up in fresh produce and animal proteins, while the larger cumulative effect may build more slowly in processed and ultra-processed foods as higher grain and ingredient costs work through manufacturing and contracts. That distinction matters for any grocer trying to protect both affordability and a health-forward brand position.</p><h3>Fresh, Healthy Foods: The Near-Term Squeeze</h3><p>The foods shoppers are most encouraged to eat, including fruits, vegetables, lean proteins, beans, and minimally processed staples, are tightly tied to current growing conditions, logistics, and energy. <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/war-iran-threatens-fresh-food-price-shock-across-developing-world-2026-03-20/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reuters</a> has already warned of a fresh-food price shock in some markets as fertilizer and transport costs climb.</p><p>Fresh produce is exposed first because it depends on fertilizer, water, labor, and energy-intensive cold chains. When fertilizer and fuel jump at the same time, growers and distributors can see higher costs immediately, especially in import-dependent categories and high-input systems such as greenhouse-grown vegetables. Animal proteins are also vulnerable because feed costs rise when fertilizer spikes push up the cost of corn and other grain inputs. As <a href="https://www.ifpri.org/blog/the-iran-wars-impacts-on-global-fertilizer-markets-and-food-production/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IFPRI</a> and <a href="https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/farmers-warn-of-food-price-spike-as-war-drives-up-fuel-and-fertilizer-costs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PBS NewsHour</a> note, that pressure can move into meat, eggs, and dairy faster than many retailers expect.</p><p>This is where the nutrition challenge begins. The <a href="https://cdn.realfood.gov/DGA.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025–2030 Dietary Guidelines for Americans</a> and related summaries from <a href="https://nutritionsource.hsph.harvard.edu/2026/01/09/dietary-guidelines-for-americans-2025-2030/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Harvard Nutrition Source</a> emphasize dietary patterns built around vegetables, fruits, whole grains, beans, nuts, and lean proteins while limiting highly processed foods. If the prices of fresh produce and lean proteins move first, shoppers can quickly feel that the healthiest basket is also becoming the hardest basket to afford.</p><p>For grocers, that means the short-term job is operational, not philosophical. Protect the visibility and affordability of healthy items before shoppers start trading down out of habit.</p><h3>Processed and Ultra-Processed Foods: The Delayed Hit</h3><p>Ultra-processed foods rely on many of the same fertilizer-intensive crops—corn, wheat, soy, and sugar—but their price path is slower. Research on <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8948822/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ultra-processed food cost structures</a> shows why: these products sit on longer chains involving mills, ingredient manufacturers, co-packers, packaging suppliers, and national brands.</p><p>When fertilizer prices rise, the first effect is usually on farm and grain markets. It then takes time for those higher input costs to move through refiners, sweetener systems, vegetable oil markets, and contract manufacturing. That lag is why processed and ultra-processed foods can appear more stable at first. Large manufacturers often have additional buffers, including hedging, long-term contracts, recipe reformulation, pack-size changes, and promotional flexibility. But as <a href="https://www.stonex.com/en/insights/fertilizer-shock-drives-new-wave-of-global-food-inflation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">StoneX</a> and <a href="https://agfundernews.com/persian-gulf-fertilizer-crisis-global-food-prices-could-rise-12-18-by-the-end-of-2026-warns-helios-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgFunderNews</a> suggest, the longer fertilizer and energy pressure lasts, the more likely processed food prices are to absorb a larger cumulative effect.</p><p>That does not mean ultra-processed foods stop looking cheap. They often remain relatively inexpensive on a per-calorie basis because they are shelf-stable, energy-dense, and built for manufacturing efficiency. But from a retailer’s point of view, that can create a dangerous middle period: fresh prices have already moved, ultra-processed prices have not fully caught up, and shoppers feel nudged toward lower-quality calories simply because they still look like the easier value.</p><h3>The Grocer’s Real Challenge</h3><p>A health-forward grocer will have to adapt twice.</p><p>The first adaptation is immediate: keep a healthy basket within reach while fresh categories are under pressure. That means leaning on resilient formats such as frozen vegetables, canned beans, no-sugar-added fruit, eggs, yogurt, and shelf-stable whole grains. It also means protecting price points on a few anchor items that help shoppers assemble affordable meals without abandoning nutrition goals.</p><p>The second adaptation comes later: prepare for a slower but broader rise in processed food costs. As higher fertilizer, grain, and energy inputs work their way through the packaged-food stack, retailers will have a chance to rebalance promotions and space. If price gaps narrow between heavily processed products and simpler alternatives, grocers can steer shoppers toward better options without asking them to pay an unrealistic premium.</p><p>This is the strategic opening hidden inside the disruption. In the short term, the mission is to stop healthy food from becoming a luxury signal. In the longer term, the mission is to use rising processed-food costs to make the shelf a little less biased toward highly engineered, heavily promoted calories.</p><h3>What Grocers Must Do Now</h3><ul><li>Protect a core affordable healthy basket, especially produce staples, frozen vegetables, canned beans, eggs, yogurt, and whole grains.</li><li>Watch fertilizer, grain, produce, and protein indicators so price moves are caught early rather than explained late.</li><li>Build meal solutions around flexible, nutrient-dense products that fit the <a href="https://cdn.realfood.gov/DGA.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dietary Guidelines</a> without relying only on premium fresh items.</li><li>Prepare for delayed inflation in packaged categories by reassessing which ultra-processed items still deserve aggressive promotion.</li><li>Use shopper messaging to make “trade sideways” choices easy, such as moving from a highly processed snack to popcorn, nuts, or a simpler whole-grain option.</li></ul><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The core thesis is simple: fresh, healthy foods are likely to feel the squeeze first, but processed foods may take the bigger hit over time. Grocers that recognize both phases early will be better positioned to keep healthy eating affordable now and reshape the value equation later.</p>$$,
    'Dave Milliken',
    'published',
    'https://media.licdn.com/dms/image/v2/D5612AQGfup8ua6ndhg/article-cover_image-shrink_720_1280/B56Z35vXtWHEAI-/0/1778011460435?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=w8XpQC1_M4S9lPb4lu4Uz0y8CHUtuacMTZ0tMuZbcvM',
    '2026-05-05T12:00:00Z',
    '2026-05-05',
    '2026-05-05T12:00:00Z',
    '2026-05-05T12:00:00Z'
  )
