INSERT INTO blog_posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, author, status, cover_image, published_date, post_date, created_at, updated_at)
  VALUES (
    'Specialty And Discount Supermarkets Are Leading Grocery''s Shocking Shakeup',
    'specialty-and-discount-supermarkets-are-leading-grocery-s-shocking-shakeup',
    'Aldi, Trader Joe''s, and digital-first grocery stores are no longer just niches. The grocery store winners in the next cycle will be the operators that sharpen their identity, build stronger reasons to return, and make value feel visible in every aisle.',
    $$<p>The Sage Take: Aldi, Trader Joe’s, and digital-first grocery stores are no longer just niches. The grocery store winners in the next cycle will be the operators that sharpen their identity, build stronger reasons to return, and make value feel visible in every aisle.</p><h3>The Latest Data Says Specialty and Discount Grocers are Winning</h3><p>A recent <a href="https://www.consumeraffairs.com/news/specialty-discount-grocers-lead-grocery-industry-shakeup-new-data-show-042326.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ConsumerAffairs</a> post highlighted new analysis showing that specialty and discount grocers are outperforming traditional supermarkets. According to the underlying <a href="https://www.bluebookservices.com/grocery-outlook-data-shows-specialty-retailers-gaining-market-share/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Consumer Edge data</a>, overall grocery spending fell about 3% year over year on a 12-month basis ended February 28, 2026, yet the bigger story is not simply reduced spending.</p><p>Shoppers are reallocating spend toward retailers that offer either superior value or a more distinctive shopping proposition. While traditional grocers like Kroger, Safeway, and regional chains are floundering, specialty chains such as Trader Joe’s, Whole Foods, and Wegmans Food Markets are drawing growth from low-, middle-, and high-income households, while traditional supermarkets are seeing pullbacks across income groups, with the sharpest declines among lower-income shoppers. On the other end of the spectrum, discount grocers, ranging from Walmart Grocery to ALDI USA, also gained overall grocery share from early 2022 through mid-2024 before that growth leveled off around mid-2025, suggesting value remains essential but is no longer the only winning formula.</p><h3>Why Shoppers Are Behaving This Way</h3><p>This shift lines up with broader consumer behavior research. In McKinsey’s <a href="https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/consumer-packaged-goods/our-insights/state-of-consumer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">State of the Consumer 2025</a> report, discount and wholesale channels continued to attract shoppers across age groups and income levels, reflecting a more deliberate definition of value. Consumers are not just trading down indiscriminately; many are making targeted trade-offs across categories so they can save in one place and spend in another.</p><p>FMI’s <a href="https://www.fmi.org/our-research/research-reports/u-s-grocery-shopper-trends" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Grocery Shopper Trends</a> reporting points in the same direction. Its 2024 shopper data showed that many consumers were actively seeking deals and adjusting shopping habits for better price value, while still protecting core categories such as fresh and organic rather than simply abandoning them. That creates a major opening for grocery retailers that can pair sharper pricing with a clear point of difference.</p><h3>The Middle Is Getting Squeezed</h3><p>The uncomfortable reality for many conventional chains is that being broadly convenient is no longer enough. Specialty formats win when they make shopping feel curated, ownable, and loyalty-worthy. Discount formats win when price leadership is obvious and operational simplicity supports it. Traditional supermarkets often struggle when they offer neither the strongest value proposition nor the most compelling experience.</p><p>This is also why private brand matters more than ever. FMI data reported by <a href="https://www.grocerydive.com/news/grocery-private-label-fmi-report-national-brands-sales-club-marketing/751705/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grocery Dive</a> found that 95% of grocery shoppers buy store brands at least occasionally, and 48% said they would still be very likely to choose store brand products even if grocery prices fall. Private label is no longer just a margin tool. It is a loyalty engine and, in many chains, one of the clearest ways to stand apart from a competitor down the road.</p><h3>What Grocery Executives Should Do Next</h3><p>For food retail leaders, the practical implication is straightforward: stop framing niche competitors as side-show players and start treating them as signals of where shopper expectations are going.</p><p>Three priorities stand out:</p><ul><li>Tighten the value story. Make pricing, promotions, and own-brand quality easier for shoppers to see and trust.</li><li>Build a differentiated identity. A grocery store needs a reason to visit beyond proximity, whether that is discovery, <a href="https://eatwithsage.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">meal solutions</a>, wellness positioning, service, or standout prepared foods.</li><li>Better use <a href="https://eatwithsage.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">grocery loyalty and personalization</a>. McKinsey has argued that stronger personalization can improve engagement and retention, which matters most when shoppers are splitting baskets across multiple banners.</li></ul><p>A useful way to think about the challenge is simple: Aldi teaches discipline, Trader Joe’s teaches distinctiveness, and the strongest legacy grocers will need both. The operators that win will be the ones that translate shopper insight into a format that feels intentional rather than generic.</p><h3>A Simple Competitive Lens</h3><p>That framework helps explain why the current grocery shakeup should not be dismissed as a temporary inflation story. Inflation may have accelerated the search for value, but the deeper lesson is that shoppers now reward clarity. They want to know why a banner deserves their trip, their basket, and their loyalty.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The old assumption was that each region had a few traditional grocery store chains, while that specialty and discount grocers were peripheral competitors. This framework is no longer accurate. The grocery store market is being reorganized around sharper retailer brand propositions, stronger private brands, and more intentional shopper choice.</p><p>For incumbents, the path forward is not imitation for its own sake. It is disciplined innovation: clearer value, better differentiation, and more meaningful brand loyalty programs. Share this article with a colleague who still believes these formats are just niche players.</p>$$,
    'Dave Milliken',
    'published',
    'https://media.licdn.com/dms/image/v2/D5612AQHPzrJz_i3nYQ/article-cover_image-shrink_720_1280/B56Z3cOTndJ4AI-/0/1777516232196?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=OMsrriMoYal0zVLreuLIaGjgxav_l40VHnO_p8hQkdU',
    '2026-04-30T12:00:00Z',
    '2026-04-30',
    '2026-04-30T12:00:00Z',
    '2026-04-30T12:00:00Z'
  )
  ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO blog_posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, author, status, cover_image, published_date, post_date, created_at, updated_at)
  VALUES (
    'The Rise of Grocery Online Ordering... And How to Build a Profitable Online Ordering Strategy',
    'rise-of-online-grocery-how-to-build-a-profitable-online-ordering-strategy',
    'Grocers face an urgent choice: build a profitable online ordering strategy, or watch digital-first competitors train your customers to shop elsewhere.',
    $$<p>Sage take: Grocers face an urgent choice: build a profitable online ordering strategy, or watch digital‑first competitors train your customers to shop elsewhere.</p><h3>The Wake‑up Call: Online Grocery Is No Longer a Side Dish</h3><p>Yesterday, <a href="https://www.grocerydive.com/news/online-grocery-sales-growth-nearly-500-billion-2028/818252/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grocery Dive</a> highlighted new findings showing that U.S. online grocery sales are projected to reach roughly 452 billion dollars by 2028, powered by rapid growth in omnichannel shopping. The <a href="https://www.fmi.org/newsroom/news-archive/view/2026/04/22/online-grocery-sales-power-omnichannel-growth-as-market-poised-to-reach--452-billion-by-2028" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FMI</a> and NielsenIQ research finds that ecommerce already accounts for nearly three‑quarters of total grocery dollar growth, even though physical stores still represent about four‑fifths of current sales.</p><p>In 2025 alone, online channels contributed close to 75% of grocery dollar growth while in‑store sales held relatively flat, underscoring that most incremental volume is now digital rather than walk‑in. The report also notes that about 94% of U.S. grocery shoppers are now <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">omnichannel</a>, routinely blending online discovery, pickup, delivery, and in‑store trips based on convenience and need.</p><p>For traditional grocers, this is the headline: Ecommerce is no longer an optional add‑on, it is the primary engine of industry growth and relevance. The question is not whether your customers will shop online, but whether they will do it profitably with you or with someone else.</p><h3>Why Online Ordering Feels Like a Margin Problem</h3><p>If you are a grocery executive, you probably don’t need to be convinced that online is growing; you feel it in your P&L. Labor for picking and packing, third‑party delivery fees, and technology costs all pile up on top of already thin grocery margins. Meanwhile, intermediaries like delivery marketplaces often own the customer relationship, squeezing your brand into a small logo inside their app.</p><p>The new FMI and <a href="https://nielseniq.com/global/en/news-center/2026/online-grocery-sales-power-omnichannel-growth-as-market-poised-to-reach-452-billion-by-2028/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NielsenIQ</a> research helps explain why this tension feels so acute. Online sales are growing at a projected annual rate of about 11.6% through 2028, while in‑store sales are expected to grow at well under 1%. That means your cost structure is being reshaped by the fastest‑growing, most operationally complex part of your business. If you treat <a href="https://www.sahmcapital.com/news/content/niq-sees-us-online-grocery-sales-hitting-452-billion-by-2028-2026-04-22" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">online ordering</a> as a bolt‑on service, or an after-thought, it will continue to erode margin and distract teams. If you treat it as a core retail format with its own economics, processes, and customer journeys, it becomes something you can actually manage and optimize.</p><h3>Principle 1: Design for Omnichannel, Not “Online vs Store”</h3><p>The new research makes a simple but crucial point: shoppers no longer choose between store and screen; they expect both. Nearly all households that buy groceries now shop both online and in‑store, picking the channel that fits that week’s schedule, budget, and energy level. The grocers who win will stop treating ecommerce as a separate business and instead design a single omnichannel experience.</p><p>In practice, that means aligning assortments, pricing, and promotions across channels, so customers don’t feel penalized for choosing curbside or delivery. It means using your app as the connective tissue for the whole journey, from list‑building and digital coupons at home to wayfinding and self‑checkout in the store. And it means measuring loyalty at the household level across channels, not in separate online and in‑store silos.</p><h3>Principle 2: Make Pickup Your Margin Anchor</h3><p>FMI’s analysis shows that shipped and delivered orders—often handled by intermediaries—now account for close to 80% of grocery ecommerce sales and are growing faster than pickup. That is a problem for grocers because those fulfillment modes tend to be costlier and harder to differentiate on. By contrast, pickup is typically more cost‑effective, easier to control end‑to‑end, and a better bridge between digital and physical.</p><p>A practical path forward is to reposition pickup as your default online ordering option and design around it. That can mean clearer incentives for customers (lower or no fees vs delivery), better pickup experience design (short wait times, clear signage, dedicated staff), and operational investments like staging areas and optimized picking routes. Over time, many retailers find that profitable ecommerce economics start with a high mix of pickup orders, supported by targeted delivery where it truly adds value.</p><h3>Principle 3: Treat the Store as Your Primary Fulfillment Asset</h3><p>Most U.S. grocery volume still runs through physical stores. That is a feature, not a bug, for online ordering. Instead of imagining separate “dark stores” for every market, many grocers will do better by making their existing stores smarter fulfillment hubs.</p><p>That starts with basics: reliable inventory accuracy, clear substitution rules, and standardized picking processes. It also includes smarter use of health and nutrition expertise. Academic medical centers and health systems have shown that food choices are deeply tied to chronic disease risk, and many are now partnering with retailers on “food as medicine” and medically tailored grocery programs. When your online experience helps customers discover healthier options, pre‑built “better for blood sugar” baskets, or dietitian‑approved swaps, you deepen loyalty and attract health‑system dollars without turning your store into a clinic.</p><h3>Principle 4: Own the Data and the Relationship</h3><p>The entities that control customer data and digital engagement will control grocery economics over the next decade. FMI’s omnichannel work emphasizes that the key strategic challenge is how well retailers “connect digital and physical experiences into one cohesive journey,” which inherently depends on first‑party data and owned digital touchpoints.</p><p>Practically, that means investing in your own app, and <a href="https://eatwithsage.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">shopper personalization</a> engine, even if you continue to partner with third‑party marketplaces. It means using visit‑and‑basket data to drive targeted offers, replenishment nudges, and cross‑category recommendations that increase both share of wallet and basket health. And it means building analytics capabilities around online order profitability by customer, channel, and fulfillment mode, so you can refine fees, promotions, and service levels with intention rather than guesswork.</p><h3>Principle 5: Start With One Clear Online Ordering Strategy Question</h3><p>Executives who move fastest on this topic don’t start with a technology RFP; they start with a blunt question: “What specific role do we want online ordering to play in our business over the next three years?” For some banners, the answer is “defend share in our core market against delivery apps.” For others, it is “acquire time‑starved young families and keep them for a decade,” or “win food‑as‑medicine partnerships with local health systems.”</p><p>Once that role is explicit, decisions about assortment, marketing, partnership, and capital allocation get much simpler. You can say no to features that don’t support that role, and yes to unglamorous but essential work—like pick‑path optimization or curbside lane design—that directly support your economic model. The evidence from FMI, NIQ, and related industry research is clear: online grocery will drive most of the growth; your job is to decide which growth you are willing to pay for and which growth must pay you back.</p><h3>Practical Implications for Grocery Leaders</h3><p>For a leadership team, this moment is less about predicting the future and more about choosing a stance. The forecasts from FMI and NIQ have grown more aggressive over time as online adoption has outpaced earlier estimates, suggesting that under‑investing in digital is now riskier than over‑investing. At the same time, uncontrolled ecommerce growth through high‑cost channels can quietly weaken your core business.</p><p>The grocers who will still be thriving in 2028 will be the ones who decide, this year, to <a href="https://thespoon.tech/study-online-grocery-sales-to-hit-143-billion-by-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">turn online ordering into a profit center</a>. They will treat digital as a core format, anchor economics in pickup, lean on their stores as fulfillment assets, and insist on owning the customer relationship and the data that comes with it. From there, technology becomes a tool, not a distraction.</p><h3>Cooking Up the Path Forward</h3><p>Online grocery is on track to add hundreds of billions of dollars in sales over the next few years, and most of that growth will accrue to retailers who build a coherent, profitable online ordering strategy. You do not need to become a Silicon Valley startup to win; you need to be clear about your role, disciplined about your economics, and obsessive about the customer journey across store and screen.</p><p>Make sure to share this new research with your leadership team. A good discussion point for your next strategy meeting is \\“What it would take for our online ordering program to be clearly profitable, and how quickly can we get there?”</p>$$,
    'Dave Milliken',
    'published',
    'https://media.licdn.com/dms/image/v2/D5612AQFX6Y63GWs10Q/article-cover_image-shrink_720_1280/B56Z3XOTeAHUAI-/0/1777432345339?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=MoCS_eeB2-vpdwEsfAwtYtqKGXvdPcxbGe7BocZIOhw',
    '2026-04-29T12:00:00Z',
    '2026-04-29',
    '2026-04-29T12:00:00Z',
    '2026-04-29T12:00:00Z'
  )
  ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;